| Average sale price in 2000 | £74,458 | £65,108 |
| Average sale price in 2025 | £275,044 | £254,514 |
| Increase in average price | £200,586 | £189,406 |
| Percentage growth | 269.4% | 290.9% |

### Which City Experienced Stronger Growth?

**Coventry recorded stronger percentage growth**, with average transaction prices increasing approximately 290.9%, compared with Birmingham's 269.4%.

That represents a difference of approximately **21.5 percentage points**.

However, Birmingham recorded a larger absolute increase in average transaction prices:

- Birmingham: £200,586 increase.
- Coventry: £189,406 increase.

Birmingham also had higher average recorded transaction prices at both the beginning and end of the period.

These results illustrate why percentage growth and absolute price increases can tell different stories about historical property market performance.

They do not establish which city offered better investment returns, because rental income, financing costs, taxation and other expenses are not included.

